WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.040
- Russia has invaded Ukraine,

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:04.700
and many are calling this "an
illegal war of aggression".

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:08.030
But what does it mean
for a war to be illegal?

00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:11.160
What does international
law mean in this context?

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:14.840
And what is even international law itself?

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:16.780
Because the recent Russian
invasion of Ukraine

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:19.230
has shocked and horrified
people all around the world.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:21.440
Almost every nation has
condemned the invasion,

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:23.180
many calling it "an illegal war".

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:25.450
Even traditionally neutral
countries like Switzerland

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:28.680
and Sweden have joined in
economic sanctions against Russia.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:30.950
And the Secretary General
of the United Nations

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:33.160
concluded that Russia's
invasion of Ukraine

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:35.697
violated international law, saying, quote,

00:00:35.697 --> 00:00:38.270
"It is wrong, it is against
the United Nations Charter.

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:39.580
It is unacceptable."

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:41.550
The United Nations Charter,
which has been affirmed

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:44.270
by 193 member states, says that countries

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:45.870
should not invade each other.

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:48.560
But what happens next comes down to this,

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:50.210
who, if anyone, has the authority

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:52.000
to enforce international law,

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.632
and how are you going to
stop an invading force?

00:00:55.632 --> 00:00:58.215
(bright music)

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:00.580
Law professor Louis Henkin,

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:03.520
who is credited founding the
study of human rights law,

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:04.787
famously wrote that, quote,

00:01:04.787 --> 00:01:06.680
"Almost all nations observe

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:08.740
almost all principles
of international law,

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:12.550
and almost all of their
obligations, almost all the time."

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:14.820
Henkin's observation was
that in the modern world,

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:16.560
most countries agree on
the general standards

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:17.990
of international conduct.

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:21.970
However, the troubles arise
because of the word, "almost."

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:23.630
What about when nations don't agree

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:25.200
with international standards of conduct,

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.900
or just simply don't observe them?

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:31.400
Do these international
standards have the force of law?

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:33.420
Does the international
community have the ability

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:35.630
to enforce its standards of conduct?

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:38.010
Well, today we're going to
talk about international law.

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:39.440
What it is, what it isn't

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:41.770
and what happens next in Ukraine.

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:44.220
But first some background on
Russian and Ukrainian history.

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:45.750
Now, this is a very complicated subject,

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:47.171
I'm only going to scratch the surface,

00:01:47.171 --> 00:01:49.240
but I think this will be helpful.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:52.440
From 1922 until 1991, Ukraine was a member

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:53.850
of the Soviet Union.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.610
But in 1991, it declared its independence

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:58.520
and became its own sovereign state.

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:00.440
In 2004, the Orange Revolution led

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:02.340
to annulling a presidential election

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.630
which was claimed to be
marred by massive corruption,

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:06.790
voter intimidation, and electoral fraud.

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:08.410
New elections were held in 2005

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:10.610
that eventually led to Viktor Yushchenko

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:12.690
beating the Russian-backed
Viktor Yanukovych.

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:16.080
But Yanukovych eventually
succeeded in the 2010 election.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:17.800
But in 2013, the Ukrainian people

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:18.830
launched a protest movement

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:22.180
against the corrupt pro-Russian
president Viktor Yanukovych,

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:24.530
who was seen as being backed
by Russian billionaires

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:25.940
who were hostile to NATO.

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:27.380
Not to make this about the United States,

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:29.080
but coincidentally, during this era,

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:31.330
Donald Trump's campaign
manager, Paul Manafort

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:34.040
spent 10 years as one of
Yanukovych's advisors.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:36.510
Of course in 2020, President
Trump was impeached

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:40.050
by the House after he
put on hold $391 million

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:42.980
in congressionally improved
military aid to Ukraine.

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:44.670
But in 2013, Ukraine's government

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:47.260
was about to sign an agreement
establishing closer ties

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:49.700
with Europe, but Yanukovych
backed out one week

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:51.460
before the agreement was to be signed.

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:52.960
Instead, he announced that Ukraine

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:55.360
would build a stronger
relationship with Russia.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:57.510
This announcement reneged
on a campaign promise

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:59.530
and Yanukovych's popularity plummeted.

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:01.240
And after the Euromaidan protests,

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:02.620
Yanukovych fled to Russia

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:05.010
and Ukraine's elected a
pro-Western government.

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:06.690
And in 2014, Russia tried to hack

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:07.970
Ukraine Central Election Commission

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:09.760
so that it would present
a far right candidate,

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:12.860
who in fact, got less than
1% of the vote as the winner.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:15.260
As historian Tim Snyder
documented, "the hack failed

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:17.500
but Russian media had been
prepared for its success

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:18.850
and Russian television went on air

00:03:18.850 --> 00:03:21.190
with falsified documents,
even digital images

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:23.580
that matched what the hack
was supposed to produce."

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:25.920
When the election gambit
failed, Russia invaded Ukraine

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:28.080
and annexed the territory of Crimea.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:31.190
And in 2019, former comedian
Volodymyr Zelenskyy,

00:03:31.190 --> 00:03:33.590
who on TV, played a
history teacher who ran

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:35.060
on an anti-corruption platform,

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:37.800
and eventually became
the president of Ukraine,

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:39.670
ran on an anti-corruption platform

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:41.210
and became the president of Ukraine.

00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:43.540
And of course, prior to
this most recent invasion,

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:45.130
Zelenskyy was famous in the United States

00:03:45.130 --> 00:03:48.620
for having what everyone
called a perfect phone call

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:50.530
with former president, Donald Trump.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:52.680
Just an absolutely perfect phone call.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:56.280
Then in February of 2022,
Russian president Vladimir Putin

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.170
launched a full scale war on Ukraine,

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:00.670
claiming that he needed
to limit Nazism in Ukraine

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:02.040
and that he was defending the people

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:04.570
of the Donbas regions of Ukraine.

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:06.820
The international community
recognized that Donbas,

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:09.490
like Crimea, is part of Ukraine.

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:11.510
So the question is, is
this an illegal war?

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:13.520
And what does it even
mean to be an illegal war

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:15.330
under international law?

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:16.480
Well, I'm sorry to tell you,

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:18.420
it probably doesn't mean all that much.

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:20.880
So the phrase "international
law" is a bit of a misnomer

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:23.350
because international
law isn't really binding

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:25.380
in the way that American
laws generally are.

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:26.860
In America, the primary sources of law

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:29.940
are the US Constitution,
laws enacted by Congress,

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:31.700
rules promulgated by federal agencies,

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:35.010
state constitutions, laws
enacted by state legislatures,

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:37.370
rules promulgated by state agencies,

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:40.170
and city or county
Charters and local laws.

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:42.550
And as see, there's generally
a hierarchy to these laws.

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:45.770
Statutes and rules can't
contradict a higher source of law.

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:47.230
For example, the US Constitution

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:49.490
is generally at the top of that list.

00:04:49.490 --> 00:04:51.610
And a state law or a state constitution

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:53.500
that contradicts the federal constitution

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:56.910
where state law is supreme
is considered void.

00:04:56.910 --> 00:04:58.640
But in contrast, there isn't really

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:00.400
an international hierarchy of law.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:03.560
Sovereign nations may agree on
general principles of conduct

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:05.610
but there isn't a body
of international law

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:08.420
that is superior to
their own national laws.

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:09.500
So the first thing you need to know

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:11.530
about international laws is
that everything is dependent

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:13.810
on the individual states,
and as you can imagine,

00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:16.020
individual sovereign
nations are incredibly

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:18.140
reluctant to give up any of their autonomy

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:20.900
and to subject themselves
to the potential whims

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:22.580
of other countries.

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:25.090
United States is no exception to this.

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:27.820
So what do we mean when we
talk about international law?

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:29.090
Well, the most important sources

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:32.220
of international law are customary
international law derived

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:34.870
from practice of the states,
treaties between the states

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:36.190
and general principles of law,

00:05:36.190 --> 00:05:38.240
recognized by civilized nations.

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:40.660
Now, the oldest source of
what we call international law

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:43.090
tends to be customary international law.

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:45.150
And this doesn't really
have a written source

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:46.870
like a criminal code.

00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:48.690
Instead, it's more like English Common Law

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:50.690
since it's made up of unwritten rules

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:53.130
that are derived from custom and practice.

00:05:53.130 --> 00:05:55.490
A rule of customary law has two elements.

00:05:55.490 --> 00:05:56.980
First, there has to be widespread

00:05:56.980 --> 00:05:58.810
and consistent state practice.

00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:00.870
The practice must be by the government

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:02.190
but must also be recognized

00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:04.490
by the state's courts and parliament.

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:07.510
And second, there has to be
what's called opinio juris,

00:06:07.510 --> 00:06:11.030
which is the state's belief
that it has a legal obligation.

00:06:11.030 --> 00:06:13.650
What's an example of
customary international law?

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:15.780
Well diplomatic immunity, for example.

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:17.700
Diplomatic immunity is
a principle that gives

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:19.180
certain foreign government officials

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:21.600
the right to safe passage
through other countries,

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:23.450
guaranteeing the diplomat cannot be sued

00:06:23.450 --> 00:06:26.410
or arrested for violating
the host country's law.

00:06:26.410 --> 00:06:27.941
This principle of diplomatic immunity

00:06:27.941 --> 00:06:30.680
is one of the oldest
elements of foreign relations

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:34.090
observed in ancient
Greece and Rome sometimes.

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:36.370
Now prior to World War
II, countries recognized

00:06:36.370 --> 00:06:38.860
diplomatic immunity based
simply on precedent.

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:40.650
But after World War
II, diplomatic immunity

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:43.610
was embedded in a number of
international conventions.

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:46.070
And today, all countries
agree that diplomats

00:06:46.070 --> 00:06:48.500
have diplomatic immunity,
although they can be

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:51.160
expelled from their host
countries for violating the law.

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:52.950
Then we can talk about treaties.

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:55.860
Treaties are a second
source of international law.

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:57.640
An international treaty
is a written agreement

00:06:57.640 --> 00:07:00.030
international law subjects
reflecting their consent

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:02.620
to the creation, alteration or termination

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:04.740
of their rights and obligations.

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:06.660
Treaties may also be called agreements,

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:08.560
conventions or protocols.

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:10.750
Now, a treaty is not
the same thing as a law.

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:12.680
A treaty is a source of an obligation

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:15.010
under the law and
treaties are only binding

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:17.780
on states which agree to
become parties to them.

00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:20.540
So there is no international tribunal

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:22.520
or government that can
force any sovereign nation

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:25.800
to become a party to a treaty
against that nation's will.

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:27.320
And so if there's no government

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:29.420
or court that can compel states to join

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:32.890
or comply with a treaty, why
are treaties binding at all?

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:34.840
Well, generally because
of a customary principle

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:37.800
of international law
called pacta sunt servada.

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:40.020
which is Latin for
"agreements must be kept".

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:42.110
And I greatly apologize for all

00:07:42.110 --> 00:07:44.540
of my mispronounced Latin
throughout this entire video.

00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:47.290
As it turns out, it's really
hard to speak a dead language.

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:49.450
Now, all states recognize
that they are bound

00:07:49.450 --> 00:07:50.680
by the agreements that they make

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:53.250
and therefore, they are
obligated to honor their treaties

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:55.730
but sometimes a treaty will
memorialize a principle

00:07:55.730 --> 00:07:57.210
of customary law, in essence,

00:07:57.210 --> 00:07:59.620
codifying a general
understanding between nations.

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:01.870
For example, the 1969 Vienna Convention

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:03.840
on the Law of Treaties codified

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:05.100
the basic customary principles

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:06.710
governing international treaties.

00:08:06.710 --> 00:08:10.240
The Vienna Convention is
known as the treaty on treaty.

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:12.940
And in the US, for example,
a treaty only becomes binding

00:08:12.940 --> 00:08:15.940
if Congress agrees that
it should become binding

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:18.650
and effectively, the law
of the United States.

00:08:18.650 --> 00:08:20.310
So again, it's about the
consent of the governed.

00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:22.410
And if a country wants
to back out of a treaty,

00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:25.840
there's effectively nothing
that any other country can do.

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:27.720
But then that takes us
to general principles

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:28.870
of civilized nations.

00:08:28.870 --> 00:08:31.060
These are general principles
of fairness and justice

00:08:31.060 --> 00:08:32.990
which are applied universally

00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:34.340
in legal systems around the world.

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:36.030
And although that sounds pretty sweeping,

00:08:36.030 --> 00:08:39.570
in reality, these principles
are pretty much gap fillers.

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:41.960
When there's a dispute
and there is no provision

00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:43.630
in an international treaty or statute

00:08:43.630 --> 00:08:46.680
or any recognized customary
principle of international law,

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:49.740
the general principles of law
can be used to fill the gaps.

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:51.280
An example of that would be the admission

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:53.250
of indirect evidence.

00:08:53.250 --> 00:08:56.370
In 1949, an International
Court of Justice decision

00:08:56.370 --> 00:08:59.250
addressed whether Albania
had civil liability

00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:01.150
for the mining of the Corfu Channel

00:09:01.150 --> 00:09:04.060
and subsequent damage to
two British Naval vessels.

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:06.210
The court rule that the UK
could establish the knowledge

00:09:06.210 --> 00:09:07.440
and responsibility of Albania

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:10.100
for laying mines in the water
through indirect evidence

00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:11.560
since indirect evidence is admitted

00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:13.587
in basically all systems of law.

00:09:13.587 --> 00:09:15.040
And then of course, there are other

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:17.140
sort of miscellaneous
sources of international law

00:09:17.140 --> 00:09:19.536
that may help resolve
international disputes.

00:09:19.536 --> 00:09:21.210
You can look to court decisions,

00:09:21.210 --> 00:09:22.610
writings by international lawyers

00:09:22.610 --> 00:09:24.620
and actions taken by the United Nations

00:09:24.620 --> 00:09:26.200
and it's many organs.

00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:28.080
But you can imagine that
if the prior sources

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:29.420
of international law aren't binding,

00:09:29.420 --> 00:09:31.770
these certainly are not
going to have that much

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:33.330
of a presidential effect.

00:09:33.330 --> 00:09:35.330
Now I'm sure many people
are watching this video

00:09:35.330 --> 00:09:36.837
and screaming at the screen,

00:09:36.837 --> 00:09:38.620
"What about the United Nations?"

00:09:38.620 --> 00:09:40.240
Well, the United Nations was established

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:42.240
after World War II to provide a formal way

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:44.130
for nations of the world to communicate

00:09:44.130 --> 00:09:45.470
and resolve disputes.

00:09:45.470 --> 00:09:47.960
The Charter of the United Nations
is an international treaty

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:50.090
that commits member states
to settle their disputes

00:09:50.090 --> 00:09:51.230
by peaceful means.

00:09:51.230 --> 00:09:53.200
Many people have the
mistaken impression though

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:55.450
that the UN General
Assembly is the government

00:09:55.450 --> 00:09:56.460
of the whole world.

00:09:56.460 --> 00:09:58.220
However, the United
Nations General Assembly

00:09:58.220 --> 00:10:01.340
has no power to legislate for
the international community.

00:10:01.340 --> 00:10:04.120
Its resolutions are not legally binding.

00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:06.760
The General Assembly cannot
enact international laws

00:10:06.760 --> 00:10:10.060
and then force member states
to submit to its authority.

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:13.330
UN resolutions do influence
international relations

00:10:13.330 --> 00:10:15.940
but they're not the equivalent
of laws and statutes.

00:10:15.940 --> 00:10:17.690
So that's the United
Nations General Assembly,

00:10:17.690 --> 00:10:19.430
but what about the UN Security Council?

00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:21.370
And in fact, the UN Security Council

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:23.410
is the only international
body that has the power

00:10:23.410 --> 00:10:26.020
to make binding decisions on all nations.

00:10:26.020 --> 00:10:27.540
The council has 15 members.

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:30.560
The five permanent members
are China, France, Russia

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:32.480
the United Kingdom and the United States.

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:34.740
And it also has 10 non-permanent members

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:37.130
whose terms run for two years.

00:10:37.130 --> 00:10:38.930
Each member has one vote.

00:10:38.930 --> 00:10:40.830
And in 2022, the temporary members

00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:43.640
of the Security Council are
the United Arab Emirates,

00:10:43.640 --> 00:10:47.250
Albania, Brazil, Gabon,
Ghana, India, Ireland,

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:49.570
Kenya, Mexico, and Norway.

00:10:49.570 --> 00:10:51.780
The five permanent members
all have veto power

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:54.420
over all UN Security Council matters.

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:56.240
And under the Charter
of the United Nations

00:10:56.240 --> 00:10:58.070
all member states are obligated to comply

00:10:58.070 --> 00:10:59.410
with Council decisions.

00:10:59.410 --> 00:11:01.340
Therefore, decisions by
the UN Security Council

00:11:01.340 --> 00:11:02.740
aren't considered legally binding.

00:11:02.740 --> 00:11:05.950
Quote under Chapter VII, "The
council can take measures

00:11:05.950 --> 00:11:09.560
to enforce decisions and ensure
that mandates are fulfilled.

00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:11.720
The Security Council can
impose economic sanctions

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:14.370
or order an arms embargo,
and on rare occasions,

00:11:14.370 --> 00:11:16.140
the Council has authorized member states

00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:17.790
to use all necessary means,

00:11:17.790 --> 00:11:19.780
including collective military action

00:11:19.780 --> 00:11:22.380
to see that its decisions
are carried out."

00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:24.480
All member states are
obligated under the UN Charter

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:26.700
to carry out the Security
Council's decision.

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:28.540
Now, all of the world is remarkably United

00:11:28.540 --> 00:11:30.600
about Russia's war against Ukraine,

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:32.670
it will still be difficult
if not impossible

00:11:32.670 --> 00:11:33.503
for the Security Council

00:11:33.503 --> 00:11:35.550
to take direct action against Russia.

00:11:35.550 --> 00:11:37.780
Most Security Councils
decisions require a majority

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:39.450
of the nine members plus consent

00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:42.240
of all five permanent member states.

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:45.230
Russia, a permanent, member
vetoed a Council resolution

00:11:45.230 --> 00:11:47.130
condemning its attack on Ukraine.

00:11:47.130 --> 00:11:49.390
China and India and the
United Arab Emirates

00:11:49.390 --> 00:11:50.720
abstained from that vote.

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:52.320
But you can imagine that as long as Russia

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:54.570
is a member of the UN Security Council,

00:11:54.570 --> 00:11:56.310
no resolution is going to pass.

00:11:56.310 --> 00:11:57.830
Though I will say there is an interesting

00:11:57.830 --> 00:11:59.420
legal movement gaining some traction,

00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:01.380
which says that Russia
is in fact not a member

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:03.150
of the UN Security Council.

00:12:03.150 --> 00:12:05.150
Effectively, the argument goes like this.

00:12:05.150 --> 00:12:07.320
The original member of
the UN Security Council

00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:10.320
was the Soviet Union and
the Soviet Union collapsed.

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:13.274
Though it was succeeded
by the Russian Federation,

00:12:13.274 --> 00:12:16.840
Russia just assumed that
it took over the spot

00:12:16.840 --> 00:12:19.480
that was originally created for the USSR.

00:12:19.480 --> 00:12:22.560
So some argue that without
formalizing this relationship

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:25.950
with Russia, Russia does not hold the seat

00:12:25.950 --> 00:12:29.300
that the former Soviet union
held on the Security Council.

00:12:29.300 --> 00:12:30.920
This seems a little bit farfetched,

00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:33.600
but we'll see if it gains
traction in the future.

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:34.560
But let's take a moment to talk

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:37.080
about the use of force
under international law.

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:38.790
After the devastation of two World Wars,

00:12:38.790 --> 00:12:40.370
United Nations was born.

00:12:40.370 --> 00:12:42.440
The UN Charter established a prohibition

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:45.320
on the use of force to
resolve disputes among states.

00:12:45.320 --> 00:12:47.840
Article 2, Section 4 of
the Charter bans the threat

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:51.170
or use of force, one, against
the territorial integrity

00:12:51.170 --> 00:12:54.070
of a state, two, against
the political independence

00:12:54.070 --> 00:12:56.480
of a state and three, in any other manner

00:12:56.480 --> 00:12:58.820
inconsistent with the purposes of the UN.

00:12:58.820 --> 00:13:00.210
Now, with that said,
there are two exceptions

00:13:00.210 --> 00:13:02.460
to the prohibition on the use of force.

00:13:02.460 --> 00:13:04.190
The Security Council can use force

00:13:04.190 --> 00:13:07.130
on behalf of the UN to
maintain peace and security.

00:13:07.130 --> 00:13:08.890
A member state can also use self-defense

00:13:08.890 --> 00:13:10.890
to repel an armed attack.

00:13:10.890 --> 00:13:12.840
Chapter VI of the UN Charter establishes

00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:14.550
that force may be used to the peace

00:13:14.550 --> 00:13:15.900
if the Security Council determines

00:13:15.900 --> 00:13:18.460
that there is the existence
of a threat to the peace,

00:13:18.460 --> 00:13:20.770
a breach of the piece
or an act of aggression

00:13:20.770 --> 00:13:23.210
and efforts to address
the matter using measures

00:13:23.210 --> 00:13:25.650
short of force have
failed or would be futile.

00:13:25.650 --> 00:13:28.040
Now, in the early days of
the UN, the Charter suggested

00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:30.390
that all member states
would make their military

00:13:30.390 --> 00:13:31.850
and resources collectively available

00:13:31.850 --> 00:13:35.000
to the Security Council
to use to stop wars.

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:37.500
But this has effectively
never materialized.

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:39.270
And as a result, when the Council needs

00:13:39.270 --> 00:13:40.820
a peacekeeping force, willing states

00:13:40.820 --> 00:13:42.630
volunteer their personnel.

00:13:42.630 --> 00:13:44.850
Putin tried to take
advantage of this exception

00:13:44.850 --> 00:13:48.380
by declaring that his forces
were a peacekeeping force

00:13:48.380 --> 00:13:50.490
and former president Trump
thought this was a great idea

00:13:50.490 --> 00:13:52.440
and suggested that the
us should invade Mexico

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:55.120
with fake peacekeeping forces as well.

00:13:55.120 --> 00:13:57.280
Of course, this is not
what peacekeeping means.

00:13:57.280 --> 00:13:59.847
Under the UN Charter as one source put it,

00:13:59.847 --> 00:14:01.240
"The Security Council takes the lead

00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:03.100
in determining the existence of a threat

00:14:03.100 --> 00:14:05.400
to the peace or an act of aggression.

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:06.340
It calls upon the parties

00:14:06.340 --> 00:14:08.050
to a dispute to settle
it by peaceful means

00:14:08.050 --> 00:14:11.210
and recommends methods of
adjustment or terms of settlement.

00:14:11.210 --> 00:14:12.900
Under Chapter VII of the UN Charter,

00:14:12.900 --> 00:14:14.840
the Security Council can
take enforcement measures

00:14:14.840 --> 00:14:17.890
to maintain or restore
international peace and security.

00:14:17.890 --> 00:14:20.310
Such measures range
from economic sanctions

00:14:20.310 --> 00:14:22.470
to international military action.

00:14:22.470 --> 00:14:25.100
The Council also establishes
UN Peacekeeping Operations

00:14:25.100 --> 00:14:26.870
and Special Political Missions."

00:14:26.870 --> 00:14:28.880
Now, additionally, Article
51 of the UN Charter

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:30.527
provides for self-defense, quote,

00:14:30.527 --> 00:14:32.329
"Nothing in the present
Charter shall impair

00:14:32.329 --> 00:14:35.399
the inherent right of individual
or collective self-defense

00:14:35.399 --> 00:14:37.890
if armed attack occurs against a member

00:14:37.890 --> 00:14:40.040
of the United Nations
until the Security Council

00:14:40.040 --> 00:14:41.890
has taken measures necessary to maintain

00:14:41.890 --> 00:14:44.390
international peace and security."

00:14:44.390 --> 00:14:46.260
Sovereign nation's right of self-defense

00:14:46.260 --> 00:14:48.010
predates international organizations,

00:14:48.010 --> 00:14:50.020
and it's one of the fundamental principles

00:14:50.020 --> 00:14:51.600
of state sovereignty.

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:53.750
And as part of this
right, nations can rescue

00:14:53.750 --> 00:14:55.730
their own citizens who
are trapped or being held

00:14:55.730 --> 00:14:58.040
in another country, but
a nation can't claim

00:14:58.040 --> 00:14:59.890
it's merely protecting its own citizens

00:14:59.890 --> 00:15:02.710
if its real aim is to
overthrow a foreign government.

00:15:02.710 --> 00:15:04.170
Now, Russian president Putin said

00:15:04.170 --> 00:15:07.280
that people in the Donbas
Republics sought Russia's help

00:15:07.280 --> 00:15:09.490
and that qualifies as self-defense.

00:15:09.490 --> 00:15:12.510
But Article 51 lines to
states not to individuals.

00:15:12.510 --> 00:15:14.720
So as of now, Russia is the only state

00:15:14.720 --> 00:15:16.800
that has recognized the
two Donbas Republics

00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:19.150
as their own nations,
though this claim appears

00:15:19.150 --> 00:15:20.890
to be pretext for invasion.

00:15:20.890 --> 00:15:22.850
And although Putin claims
that the Ukrainians

00:15:22.850 --> 00:15:24.625
are committing genocide in the Donbas,

00:15:24.625 --> 00:15:27.430
this also appears to be a pretext as well.

00:15:27.430 --> 00:15:30.120
And Russia also claims that
Ukraine is the aggressor here,

00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:32.070
but even after or the invasion of Crimea,

00:15:32.070 --> 00:15:35.720
Ukraine did not invade or
really even threaten Russia.

00:15:35.720 --> 00:15:39.050
So is the war in Ukraine
illegal under international law?

00:15:39.050 --> 00:15:41.290
Well, yes, but the
principle that war's illegal

00:15:41.290 --> 00:15:42.990
is only as strong as the
international community's

00:15:42.990 --> 00:15:45.410
willingness to intervene in the conflict.

00:15:45.410 --> 00:15:47.400
And the first modern
attempt to make war illegal

00:15:47.400 --> 00:15:49.930
was in 1928, when the
US and France created

00:15:49.930 --> 00:15:52.830
the Kellogg-Briand Pact,
where dozens of countries

00:15:52.830 --> 00:15:54.690
joined the treaty which called on nations

00:15:54.690 --> 00:15:56.720
to settle their disputes peacefully.

00:15:56.720 --> 00:15:58.060
This treaty was only as good

00:15:58.060 --> 00:15:59.780
as its members' commitment to it.

00:15:59.780 --> 00:16:01.950
And of course, the pact meant nothing

00:16:01.950 --> 00:16:04.440
when World War II erupted a decade later.

00:16:04.440 --> 00:16:07.460
The world tried again in 1949
with the Geneva Conventions.

00:16:07.460 --> 00:16:10.130
The Geneva Conventions
govern how civilians,

00:16:10.130 --> 00:16:12.270
prisoners of war and soldiers are treated.

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:14.040
You've probably heard that
the Geneva Conventions

00:16:14.040 --> 00:16:15.780
prohibit nations from torturing people

00:16:15.780 --> 00:16:17.970
or using torture to extract information.

00:16:17.970 --> 00:16:19.520
And effectively, all nations have joined

00:16:19.520 --> 00:16:22.300
the Geneva Conventions, but
these treaties only govern

00:16:22.300 --> 00:16:23.860
how people are treated during war,

00:16:23.860 --> 00:16:24.720
they don't say anything

00:16:24.720 --> 00:16:27.000
about the legality of the war itself.

00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:28.980
And even though the
Security Council actions

00:16:28.980 --> 00:16:31.040
are said to be binding,
they're only binding

00:16:31.040 --> 00:16:33.590
if the Council actually
choose to enforce them.

00:16:33.590 --> 00:16:35.720
For example, the
Iran-Iraq War in the 1980s

00:16:35.720 --> 00:16:37.460
clearly violated the laws of war

00:16:37.460 --> 00:16:40.500
but the Security Council
took no steps to intervene.

00:16:40.500 --> 00:16:42.220
And then finally, perhaps most importantly

00:16:42.220 --> 00:16:44.100
there is the international Criminal Court.

00:16:44.100 --> 00:16:46.730
Now, the ICC is an independent
judicial institution

00:16:46.730 --> 00:16:48.460
that was established in 2002.

00:16:48.460 --> 00:16:51.310
It is empowered to investigate
and prosecute war crimes,

00:16:51.310 --> 00:16:52.850
crimes against humanity, genocide

00:16:52.850 --> 00:16:54.250
and the crime of aggression.

00:16:54.250 --> 00:16:56.950
123 countries are ICC members,

00:16:56.950 --> 00:16:59.410
which means that these
countries have given the ICC

00:16:59.410 --> 00:17:01.450
the authority to investigate
and prosecute crimes

00:17:01.450 --> 00:17:05.400
committed by their nations or
by anyone on their territory.

00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:07.890
The ICC doesn't have
the power over countries

00:17:07.890 --> 00:17:09.760
that aren't a party to
the treaty, however.

00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:13.090
And the US, for example,
did not join the ICC.

00:17:13.090 --> 00:17:15.130
President Clinton signed the Rome Statute,

00:17:15.130 --> 00:17:17.170
the treaty authorizing the ICC,

00:17:17.170 --> 00:17:18.870
but the Senate wouldn't ratify it.

00:17:18.870 --> 00:17:21.870
And president George W. Bush
then unsigned the treaty,

00:17:21.870 --> 00:17:24.010
informing the UN that
America did not recognize

00:17:24.010 --> 00:17:26.460
any obligations under the ICC.

00:17:26.460 --> 00:17:28.550
Nevertheless, US citizens
can still be prosecuted

00:17:28.550 --> 00:17:30.130
for committing war crimes if it happens

00:17:30.130 --> 00:17:32.490
in a territory that is an ICC member.

00:17:32.490 --> 00:17:34.850
Afghanistan, for example,
is a party to the ICC

00:17:34.850 --> 00:17:37.580
and can request that the
ICC investigate Americans

00:17:37.580 --> 00:17:39.470
for committing war crimes,
but at the same time,

00:17:39.470 --> 00:17:42.720
the ICC cannot override the
laws of any sovereign nation.

00:17:42.720 --> 00:17:44.750
It only exercises its
jurisdiction when a country

00:17:44.750 --> 00:17:47.488
is either unwilling or
genuinely unable to investigate

00:17:47.488 --> 00:17:49.300
and prosecute war crimes.

00:17:49.300 --> 00:17:50.970
And of course, countries
are free to prosecute

00:17:50.970 --> 00:17:52.750
war crimes in their own courts.

00:17:52.750 --> 00:17:54.810
Though, that being said,
neither Russia nor Ukraine

00:17:54.810 --> 00:17:57.050
have signed or adopted the Rome Treaty

00:17:57.050 --> 00:17:59.190
and are not members of the ICC.

00:17:59.190 --> 00:18:02.260
So Russia cannot be prosecuted
for the crime of aggression.

00:18:02.260 --> 00:18:05.350
However, in 2014, after
Russia annexed Crimea,

00:18:05.350 --> 00:18:08.500
Ukraine submitted a declaration
giving the ICC jurisdiction

00:18:08.500 --> 00:18:10.680
for crimes committed any
time on it's property

00:18:10.680 --> 00:18:12.880
after February, 2014.

00:18:12.880 --> 00:18:16.080
So Ukraine can ask that
ICC prosecutors open

00:18:16.080 --> 00:18:19.360
a full investigation
into Russia's actions.

